<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nguyen Phu Trong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/2024">2024</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nguyễn Phú Trọng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nguyen Phu Trong was general secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a> from 2011 until his death in 2024, and also president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> from 2018 to 2021. A Marxist theorist, he led a sweeping anti-corruption campaign known as the 'blazing furnace' that removed many senior officials, and he balanced Vietnam's relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nguyen-phu-trong.jpg" alt="Nguyen Phu Trong" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Schultz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nguyen_Phu_Trong_HN2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nguyễn Phú Trọng was born on 14 April 1944 in Đông Hội Commune, Đông Anh district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. His official biography gives his family background only as average peasant. He took a bachelor's degree in philology at Vietnam National University, Hanoi, between 1963 and 1967, and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a> on 19 December 1967.</p><p>He worked for the party's theoretical journal Tạp chí Cộng Sản in several periods between 1968 and 1996. In 1981 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> to study at the Academy of Sciences and received a Candidate of Sciences degree in history in 1983; he became an Associate Professor in 1992 and a Professor in 2002.</p><p>Trọng joined the party's Central Committee in January 1994, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> in December 1997 and the National Assembly in May 2002. From January 2000 to June 2006 he was secretary of the party committee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, the de facto head of the city authority, and on 26 June 2006 he was elected Chairman of the National Assembly, a post he held until 2011.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Trọng was elected General Secretary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-vietnam">Communist Party of Vietnam</a> at the 11th National Congress in 2011 and re-elected in 2016 and again on 31 January 2021, when he became the first leader since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-duan">Lê Duẩn</a> to serve more than two terms. Soon after taking office he became head of the Central Steering Committee for Anti-Corruption, which in 2012 was placed directly under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>. A large anti-corruption campaign followed, which he compared to a burning furnace; between 2017 and 2020 the Politburo took disciplinary action against 110 senior party members, and the president Nguyễn Xuân Phúc later resigned after scandals involving his subordinates.</p><p>In foreign policy he visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in October 2011, and in July 2015 he became the first Vietnamese party chief to make a state visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Following the death of President Trần Đại Quang, the National Assembly elected him president on 23 October 2018, making him the third person after Hồ Chí Minh and Trường Chinh to head both party and state. He held the presidency until 2 April 2021, when Nguyễn Xuân Phúc succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 18 July 2024 Trọng was replaced on a caretaker basis as General Secretary by President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/to-lam">Tô Lâm</a> because of ill health, and he died on 19 July 2024 at Central Military Hospital 108 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, aged 80. A state funeral was held on 25 and 26 July, ending with his burial at Mai Dịch Cemetery. </p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nguyễn Phú Trọng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Van Linh (1986–1991), the architect of the reform era, Do Muoi, Le Kha Phieu, Nong Duc Manh and <b>Nguyen Phu Trong</b> (2011–2024), who led a sweeping anti-corruption campaign known as the ‘blazing furnace’ that forced out senior officials, including two state presidents; To Lam succeeded him in 2024.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Đông Anh. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.02,105.84&amp;year=1996" aria-label="Hanoi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Nguyen Phu Trong"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3881 5695h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3881 5695h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=21.02,105.84&amp;year=1996">What was here in 1996? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
